        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The rule is 50°F and rising, sustained for 24 to 48 hours including overnight, measured on the asphalt rather than just in the air. Every part of that sentence is doing work, and the overnight low is the part people skip.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Below that threshold, asphalt emulsion cannot coalesce into a film. What you get is a chalky, weakly bonded layer that stays water-soluble, washes away in the first rain, or survives the winter only to lift in sheets the following spring. It is not a matter of the coat being slightly less good — it is a coat that never became a coat.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            If your window is marginal, wait. An unsealed driveway passes one more winter without meaningful harm, while a failed cold-weather seal costs you the product, a cleanup job, and the same weekend’s work again in spring. In the meantime, fill the cracks — most fillers tolerate cooler conditions than surface sealer, and keeping water out of the base through winter is the more valuable job anyway.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>
